{:edb/db-before-t 2418 :edb/db-after-t 2419 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535731 50 #inst "2026-10-09T06:08:55.160Z" 13194139535731 true] [17592186171987 1020 #edb/ref 17592186363809 13194139535731 true] [17592186171987 1020 #edb/ref 17592186363837 13194139535731 true] [17592186172030 1020 #edb/ref 17592186363816 13194139535731 true] [17592186172030 1020 #edb/ref 17592186363823 13194139535731 true] [17592186172030 1020 #edb/ref 17592186363830 13194139535731 true] [17592186363809 1017 #edb/ref 17592186363810 13194139535731 true] [17592186363809 1089 "q-125738" 13194139535731 true] [17592186363809 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535731 true] [17592186363809 1100 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨2x{y}^{2},\\,2y{x}^{2}⟩$ and the points $A(1,1)$ and $B(2,8)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $F$ is conservative.\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=255,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=255,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{3},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$" 13194139535731 true] [17592186363809 1113 #uuid "1b95d172-b59b-523e-976b-b07dae77fe4b" 13194139535731 true] [17592186363809 1145 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,8)-f(1,1) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}-{1}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 64-1 \\\\  & =255.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=255.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535731 true] [17592186363810 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363811 13194139535731 true] [17592186363810 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363812 13194139535731 true] [17592186363810 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363813 13194139535731 true] [17592186363810 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363814 13194139535731 true] [17592186363810 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363815 13194139535731 true] [17592186363810 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535731 true] [17592186363810 1041 "selection" 13194139535731 true] [17592186363810 1104 #edb/ref 17592186363811 13194139535731 true] [17592186363810 1110 #uuid "eebfc9ac-8103-5a63-9e56-25bf3f68b374" 13194139535731 true] [17592186363811 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535731 true] [17592186363811 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535731 true] [17592186363811 1111 #uuid "27fc9a35-f421-57b5-bdef-edabc4282bda" 13194139535731 true] [17592186363812 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535731 true] [17592186363812 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535731 true] [17592186363812 1111 #uuid "4156b875-2ee5-5782-a700-8cc2fc200c44" 13194139535731 true] [